{# =====================================================================
   SNMPv3 agent. INCLUDED LAST BY br.rsc.j2, er.rsc.j2 AND site.rsc.j2.

   LAST ON PURPOSE, and it's the only block in this repo placed by that
   argument. /import is strictly ordered (AD-LOG §20.2) and the deploy path
   runs it straight after reset-configuration (README): a line RouterOS
   rejects doesn't merely fail, it abandons every line below it on a box that
   has already been wiped. The two properties here whose ACCEPTED VALUES vary
   across RouterOS versions (authentication-protocol and encryption-protocol
  ) are therefore the last thing in the file. Get them wrong and you lose
   monitoring, which you find out about from the outside; anywhere else in the
   file you would lose the firewall, which you wouldn't.

   Monitoring is the canary. You put the canary at the end of the mine, not
   at the entrance.

   The input-chain accept and the `snmp` address-list are deliberately NOT
   here. They live with the rest of the firewall, where their position
   relative to the terminal drop is visible on the page.

   Needs `t`, `r.fqdn` and `r.snmp_location`.
   ===================================================================== #}
{% if t.snmp is defined and t.snmp %}

# =====================================================================
#  SNMP: read-only monitoring plane
# =====================================================================
# --- the v1/v2c default, armed or not --------------------------------
# RouterOS ships exactly one community: name="public", security=none. Any host
# the firewall lets through can read the entire MIB (interfaces, addresses,
# neighbours, routes, serial numbers) using a string that has been the same
# on every device on earth since 1988. On a CHR whose ether1 faces the DFZ
# that isn't an information leak, it's a UDP amplifier with someone else's
# name on the return address.
#
# read-access=no rather than remove: /snmp trap-community still points at
# "public" by default, and removing a referenced object is exactly the kind of
# line whose behaviour varies by version. Denying it read is total (the
# community can no longer answer anything) and it can't fail.
:if ([:len [/snmp/community/find where name="public"]] > 0) do={ /snmp/community/set [find where name="public"] read-access=no write-access=no }
{% if t.snmp.enabled %}

# --- the one v3 user -------------------------------------------------
# security=private is RouterOS for authPriv: authenticated AND encrypted.
# There's no v2c fallback configured anywhere, so a poller that can't do v3
# doesn't get a degraded answer. It gets no answer.
#
# read-access=yes, write-access=no. The write side isn't theoretical on
# RouterOS: `snmpset` to 1.3.6.1.4.1.14988.1.1.7.1.0 reboots the router, and
# 1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0 renames it. Monitoring needs neither.
#
# remove-then-add, matching the ssh-key import in _mgmt.j2, so that re-running
# this file against a LIVE router replaces the entry instead of failing on a
# duplicate name. The remove is a no-op when nothing matches.
#
# The passphrases below come from keys.json, never from topology.yml. They are
# 32 URL-safe characters, which is also why they can be quoted here without
# any escaping worth thinking about.
/snmp/community/remove [find where name="{{ t.snmp.user }}"]
/snmp/community/add name={{ t.snmp.user }} security=private read-access=yes write-access=no \
    authentication-protocol={{ t.snmp.auth_protocol }} authentication-password="{{ t.snmp.auth_password }}" \
    encryption-protocol={{ t.snmp.encryption_protocol }} encryption-password="{{ t.snmp.priv_password }}"

# location is this router's own site, not a fleet-wide constant. See the
# note in topology.yml. sysName is already /system/identity ({{ r.fqdn }}).
#
# engine-id-suffix IS THE LINE THAT MAKES MORE THAN ONE ROUTER POLLABLE.
# RouterOS does NOT derive a per-device engine-id: with the suffix empty it's
# 80003a8c04 (MikroTik's enterprise number, text format, nothing after it) on
# every box, measured on 7.24.5 across all four routers. SNMPv3 keys its
# time-window state (engineBoots/engineTime) by engine-id, so a poller that
# sees four agents claiming one identity keeps the clock of whichever answered
# first and discards every other router's replies as outside the window.
# Zabbix reports that as "Timeout while connecting", indistinguishable from a
# firewall drop. The symptom is exactly one host working.
#
# The suffix is the router's identity: already unique fleet-wide (DNS and
# keys.json both depend on it), it survives a CHR rebuild, and it makes
# `/snmp print` say which box it's. generate.py holds it to the 27 octets an
# engine-id has room for after the 5-octet prefix. CHANGING IT on a live box
# means restarting zabbix-server, which caches engine state in-process.
#
# NO TRAPS. trap-target stays at its 0.0.0.0 default, so this agent never
# sends anything unasked. Zabbix polls; a v3 trap would additionally require
# the RECEIVER to be taught each router's engine-id. Per-router state to
# maintain in exchange for duplicating data we already collect on a 1m interval.
/snmp
set enabled=yes engine-id-suffix="{{ r.fqdn }}" contact="{{ t.snmp.contact }}" location="{{ r.snmp_location }}"
{% else %}

# NOT ARMED (snmp.enabled: false in topology.yml). Two deliberate effects, and
# both of them are worth having on their own:
#   - the agent is turned off EXPLICITLY, not left at whatever the default is
#   - no credential is rendered into this file at all, so a pass-1 .rsc that
#     ends up somewhere it shouldn't leaks nothing
# Flip snmp.enabled, put the poller in snmp.sources, rebuild, redeploy.
/snmp
set enabled=no
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
